+### Incoming workflow starts
+
+| Method | Path | Description |
+|---|---|---|
+| `GET` | `/api/v1/triggers` | List the caller's starts. It reports whether a private address exists, never the address or its secret. |
+| `POST` | `/api/v1/triggers` | Bind exactly one saved template or published flow. The response contains `direct_webhook_url` once. |
+| `POST` | `/api/v1/triggers/{name}/direct-address` | Replace the private address. The old one stops working immediately; the new address is returned once. |
+| `DELETE` | `/api/v1/triggers/{name}` | Remove the start and revoke its private address. |
+| `POST` | `/webhook/trigger/{opaque-token}` | Start the bound work from JSON (`application/json` or `+json`) or URL-encoded form data, up to 256 KiB. |
+
+The opaque address is a bearer credential: whoever has it can start that one
+registered workflow. Store it privately, use HTTPS, and replace it if exposed.
+Lightwork's built-in web-server access log redacts the private path and query;
+configure any hosting gateway or reverse-proxy logs to redact it as well.
+The endpoint does not require the deployment-wide webhook signing secret or a
+Lightwork-specific signing relay. The existing signed `/webhook/run` format
+remains available. A supplied `Idempotency-Key`, `X-Event-ID`,
+`X-GitHub-Delivery`, or body event ID is hashed before bounded replay
+protection; a sender that supplies no event ID has at-least-once delivery.
+For an object with a `data` object, that object supplies the workflow input;
+otherwise the full posted object does. Execution still uses the trigger's
+saved owner, workspace, exact flow publication, current permission checks and
+configured spending/rate limits. It does not verify provider-specific event
+signatures.
