+gets a first-step answer instead of authorization language. If no AI service is
+connected, an administrator receives a direct Settings link; a non-admin is
+told to ask their administrator and return to try a sample task. Unrelated
+page suggestions no longer replace this answer. Three focused local tests
+passed. Live provider setup and installed-customer acceptance remain open.
+
+October 8 UX-201 custom spending cap: invalid amounts now trigger another
+prompt; they cannot silently become the default or leave a negative cap in the
+saved configuration. The focused regression test tried “ten dollars,” “-3,”
+“NaN,” and then “$10”; only the explicit $10 was saved. Local test passed.
+
+October 8 UX-234 headless settings import: Lightwork now checks settings before
+writing, leaves the installed file untouched when a setting is unusable, and
+retains a restricted backup before replacing valid settings. Four focused
+local cases passed.
+
+October 8 UX-013 host Git filter execution: coding-mode patch plumbing now
+checks agent-writable repository metadata before host-side Git operations and
+refuses worktrees with executable filters; guarded calls run with a scrubbed
+environment. Sixteen focused local regression cases passed.
+
+October 8 UX-174 component import errors: the CLI now preserves the actual
+dependency failure when an installed dashboard or setup component cannot load,
+while keeping the reviewed-checkout instruction for a component that is truly
+missing. Two focused local cases passed.
+
+October 8 UX-425 customer help and public docs: public Troubleshooting and
+Glossary pages now explain common setup, task, workflow, approval, access, and
+recovery questions in plain language. Product links on Chat, tasks, results,
+approvals, workflows, people, workspaces, Settings, AI services, and Connections
+now lead to the Use Lightwork guide. Local navigation/allowlist validation
+confirmed all 52 public pages have matching entries and sources, and the strict
+local public-site build passed. Per-deployment documentation URLs remain open.
+
+October 8 UX-378 Chat search: the Chat sidebar now searches task titles,
+requests, saved results and retained user/assistant conversation messages as
+the person types. Results show a matching excerpt and update date, with an
+explicit continuation for older work. The cursor remains bound to the current
+account and workspace. Search uses current-access checks and no-store
+responses; a changed task or access clears private matches. Internal work logs
+and hidden system/tool messages remain outside this search. Focused local API,
+browser-controller, localization and Ruff checks passed; full ChatGPT/Claude
+feature parity remains open.
+
+October 8 UX-413: the conversation page now names the current chat in a
+screen-reader heading. Its answer renderer already starts Markdown headings
+below the page heading; the focused rendered-page check now guards that
+relationship. The print-only heading remains separate.
+
+October 8 flow-approval failure copy: approval preview and resume now hide
+workflow-engine details. Invalid decisions return a 400 input message, an
+unavailable authority gets a temporary-check message, and stale reviews direct
+the person to refresh and review again. Three focused local API cases passed;
+no external effect or customer installation was exercised.
+
+October 8 guided-workflow cleanup: the compliance scoping, scope-actions,
+privacy notice, privacy request and one-time data-delivery pages no longer
+expose generic operation selectors or JSON request editors. Their named
+assessment, scope, mapping, approval, task, recovery, report and privacy actions
+remain in the ordinary forms and record lists. Fifteen focused rendered-page
+tests and seventeen existing browser-workflow tests passed. This does not
+establish UI coverage for every API operation or qualify a customer
+installation.
+
+October 8 support records: nine AI, governance, document, inventory and vendor
+pages keep detailed responses out of sight and offer a local support-record
+download after a response exists. Their plain-language result stays visible;
+the page warns that a downloaded record may include workspace details. Safe
+filenames are offered through the browser, without claiming the browser saved
+them. One hundred fifteen focused browser-client cases, including each page's
+download action, and 80 rendered-page cases passed locally. This adds a
+customer-controlled support path; it does not guarantee browser storage or
+that every product action has an audit record.
+
+October 8 nested workflow pauses: the initial release guard refused every pause
+inside an included workflow. The implementation now supersedes that guard for
+direct approval, timer and external-event pauses: it persists the root and child
+frames, binds approval review to the pinned child path, and resumes without
+replaying completed work. Pauses inside repeated, parallel and failure-handling
+bodies still fail publication because those containers do not have a durable
+resume contract. Focused local queue/API journeys are recorded with the current
+implementation; shared-storage and live customer acceptance remain open.
+
+October 8 Copilot workflow drafting: from another accessible page, Copilot can
+route a new-workflow request to the Steps designer and carry the original brief
+in a short-lived, one-time tab handoff. The handoff is bound to the requesting
+account/workspace and exact destination; the designer rejects a mismatch and
+keeps any existing draft. A temporary failure to check the account keeps the
+brief in the tab for a safe retry. It only fills the description: the person
+still chooses **Draft workflow**, and nothing is generated, saved or published
+by the handoff. Focused local API, Copilot and designer handoff checks passed.
+This does not qualify phrase matching for every request, a live provider, or an
+installed customer journey.
+
+October 8 workflow-authoring spend controls: visual-flow drafting and editing,
+plus template and specialist-playbook drafting from a brief, file, or refinement,
+now share the task-start admission checks for emergency stop, per-person quota,
+spending limits, workspace allowance and tenant status. Every provider send
+rechecks current authority, uses the configured role and the lower of the
+operator's cap or the configurable authoring ceiling (default $0.50), allows one
+physical provider attempt, settles usage to the existing ledger, and records
+start/completion activity without recording the private brief. Both the
+authoring ceiling and the maximum per-run limit AI may set on a new workflow are
+editable in Settings and collected by the installer. Personal and workspace task
+limits continue to apply. A blocked start or dispatch returns a plain
+explanation before changing the draft. Focused local checks cover admission,
+settings-driven limits, settlement, activity records and emergency-stop refusal;
+live spend and a customer installation remain unverified.
+
+October 8 Chat recents: a conversation paused on an unanswered question now says
+“Needs your answer,” while the other run states keep their plain-language labels.
+The status is available to assistive technology and follows question refreshes
+and live run updates for the open chat. Focused local server and browser-controller
+checks passed; installed-customer acceptance remains open.
+
+October 8 Windows language-server timeout: Windows anonymous pipes cannot be
+waited on with `select`, and the prior fallback blocked in `read1` past the
+configured deadline. The LSP bridge now checks pipe availability with
+`PeekNamedPipe` and keeps waiting only until the deadline. The regression
+scenario uses a silent two-second server and asserts a 0.2-second timeout
+returns within 1.5 seconds. All 12 LSP bridge tests, Ruff and `git diff --check`
+passed locally on Windows; no hosted CI or customer installation was used.
+
+October 8 Copilot answer-check selection: the reviewed operation now accepts
+the same full set of twenty customer-authored questions as Workforce. The
+review shows the matching per-question spend ceiling before confirmation (up
+to $1.00 for twenty questions); exact question versions, configured specialist
+model, no-tool execution and no-retry recovery remain enforced. Focused local
+Copilot tests cover twenty, six and three selected questions without sending a
+provider request during review.
+
+October 9 workflow test examples: builders can save named inputs from the last
+safe draft test, then start up to twenty saved examples against the current
+draft from the designer. Each run uses mock executors and links to the normal
+run record. Tenant/workflow access, typed input validation, stale list
+revisions, duplicate-safe save and batch retry, and zero provider calls are
+covered by one focused local API journey; all 55 flow-input browser-controller
+checks pass. The UI says the mocks do not judge answer quality and asks people
+to use fictional data. Single-step/live connector testing, expected-output
+checks and installed-customer acceptance remain open.
+# October 9 follow-through
+
+The flow action picker now preserves the connector catalog's category labels
+and presents common product names correctly (GitHub, HubSpot, ServiceNow, HTTP,
+OAuth, and API). A focused browser-controller check exercises the rendered
+picker. This improves the customer-facing label only; it does not alter the
+underlying action contract.
+
+Saved workflow test examples are now authenticated-encrypted and cryptographically
+bound to both the active tenant and the workflow. Existing plaintext examples
+remain readable and migrate to sealed storage on their next change. The focused
+API journey checks tamper refusal and prevents moving a saved file to another
+workflow from exposing its contents.
+
+The run page now offers **Edit answers and test again** for a finished safe
+workflow test. It opens the same workflow with editable answers from that
+dry-run, leaves redacted values blank, and starts only the mock-only draft test
+after the person reviews the form. It refuses live runs and runs from other
+workflows; grouped or unusually large values are called out instead of being
+silently reshaped. Focused local browser checks cover the run-page action,
+changed inputs and the refusal boundaries. The existing local taught-step
+check now accepts an optional expected answer and retains an exact match or
+mismatch with the original result. The UI states that this is the user's own
+assertion, not independently verified correctness. Real connected-action
+output checks and saving a live connector response as sample data remain open.
+
+The workflow designer can also compare safe examples against the exact published
+version and current draft. It reads a bounded summary of each owner's paired
+runs and displays whether the recorded step paths and end states match, with
+links to inspect each run. The comparison is limited to recorded step status;
+it does not compare answer content, prove correctness, evaluate knowledge
+sources, or judge a difference as a regression. The summary returns no inputs,
+prompts, connector arguments or step outputs. Local API coverage checks owner
+access, batch pairing and the private-data boundary; customer deployment and
+live AI or connector behavior remain unqualified.
+
+The Chat emergency stop now stays in the header instead of inside the
+collapsible navigation drawer, so it remains reachable while a person is
+watching a response on a phone. Focused local shell checks verify header
+placement and its accessible action name; no hosted CI or live stop action was
+run.
+
+The consent-application manifest scanner now catches DTD and entity
+declarations in UTF-16 and UTF-32 as well as UTF-8 before parsing. Four focused
+cases cover both byte orders, closing a parser-hardening bypass without adding
+a runtime dependency.
+
+Consent enforcement now recalculates the receipt digest and checks the
+immediate per-profile predecessor for every receipt considered under the
+active policy. If stored choices or their chain do not match, the decision
+fails closed with the existing customer-facing integrity explanation. Focused
+local checks cover edited choices and a re-sealed receipt with a broken chain;
+the work does not claim an external root of trust or a complete historical
+chain report.
+
+Retirement now reports erasure only after its disposal handler succeeds.
+Missing handlers or subject scope and partial failures leave the recorded
+`erased` value false; failure details use safe messages instead of raw
+exception text. Archive failures also keep raw exception text out of the signed
+record, and a missing archive handler is shown as incomplete. Sixteen focused
+local tests cover these paths. The lifecycle operation still has no customer
+UI or CLI entry point, so customer-operated retirement remains unimplemented.
+
+The Homebrew documentation no longer directs customers to a missing tap or a
+formula with a placeholder checksum. It states that this distribution path is
+unavailable and points to supported installation options until maintainers
+publish and verify the tap.
+
+Completed agent answers in workflow run details now open in the shared safe
+Markdown renderer. **Show answer as written** switches back to the exact saved
+text. Named values remain expandable and retain their paging limits; installed
+customer rendering has not been verified.
+
+UX-292 follow-through: the self-improvement research experiment now uses
+customer-facing language on the Learning page, states that results require
+human review and that no changes are installed automatically, and explains
+where to review its safeguards, including the specific missing prerequisites.
+Toggle failures no longer expose internal
+experiment or deployment-setting names, config paths, or raw exception text;
+unexpected failures include a support reference and warn the person to check
+the saved state before retrying. Eleven focused local cases pass. UX-292
+remains open for other dynamic errors and direct instructions.
+
+October 9 Copilot follow-through: a question asked from Chat now finds a
+matching authored answer on another visible page when exactly one task matches
+and the caller meets that task's permission requirement. The ChatGPT
+subscription question explains the existing Codex sign-in path and opens the
+exact Settings control without sending the question to a model. Focused API
+coverage includes restricted permissions; a local browser journey follows the
+answer link into Settings. During initial regression reproduction, the
+configured model path was attempted and failed with `APIConnectionError`; no
+answer was received. Final tests used provider-free behavior and an isolated
+browser fixture that blocks outbound calls. No hosted CI was used.
+
+October 9 reviewed task-start recovery: if the final access check changes after
+Lightwork claims a reviewed Copilot task but before its background worker is
+scheduled, the exact tenant-scoped receipt and goal now return atomically to a
+recoverable draft. An exact same-operation API retry queues that task once and
+reports it as newly queued. Copilot identifies this known refusal as “Nothing
+was started,” preserves the entered task details, and opens a fresh current
+review that needs a new confirmation. Focused local API, receipt and in-product
+control checks pass. This does not resolve general pending-goal recovery across
+process and queue failures. No hosted CI or paid provider call was used.
+
+October 9 task-handoff receipts: Chat and idempotent REST submissions now
+record when worker handoff begins and whether the dispatcher confirmed
+acceptance. Queue acceptance appears in the task page as waiting for a worker.
+An unavailable or failed handoff is retained as requiring a status check;
+replaying the same submission cannot silently dispatch it again. The periodic
+sweep moves a stale handoff receipt to the same status-check state, leaves the
+pending goal untouched, and never retries work. If the task itself advances,
+the receipt records that saved status even when the runner reply is missing or
+late. The task's encrypted activity stream records each claimed, confirmed,
+uncertain or observed handoff in the same transaction as the durable receipt;
+an activity-write failure rolls the transition back. Focused local
+submission-store, activity API, atomicity, orphan-sweeper and Chat journeys
+cover accepted, uncertain, stale and replay paths. This improves customer
+visibility and duplicate protection but does not yet reconcile durable
+ownership across Redis, gRPC or non-submission dispatch paths. No hosted CI or
+paid provider call was used.
+
+October 9 published Studio reports: report views now read retained observations
+from the current approved continuous-evidence definitions and active
+enrollments, apply saved columns, filters, grouping and sorting, and refuse
+fields outside the viewer's sensitivity access. Results are read-only, bounded,
+and explicitly described as technical evidence rather than a compliance
+conclusion. The focused local service check covers current-source binding,
+filtering, projection and restricted-field refusal; Node syntax and Ruff checks
+also pass. Generic evidence collection writes and external report destinations
+remain open. No hosted CI or paid provider call was used.
+
+October 9 Copilot report access: the current published report is now a
+permission-checked read-only Copilot action. The person deliberately chooses a
+report, sees its bounded results in an accessible table, and gets a clear note
+when rows, fields, groups or sources are omitted. Copilot checks access to both
+the design and evidence pages and rechecks the exact active version before
+reading. The action reads saved results only; it does not start a collection or
+send those results to an AI service.
+Focused local service, registry and browser interaction checks passed; the full
+saved view remains in the native report page. No hosted CI or paid provider call
+was used.
+
+Native and Copilot report reads now write a confirmed Activity log receipt before
+returning results. The customer-facing entry identifies the actor, report
+version, result and source counts, and access path while omitting row values,
+filters and evidence content. If the receipt cannot be confirmed, the results
+are withheld. The record shows that the server prepared a response, not that a
+person saw it.
+
+October 9 UX-292 follow-through: SAML metadata and sign-in failures now return a
+plain recovery step and a support reference instead of exposing optional-library
+or provider setup details. Disabled OpenAI- and Claude-compatible gateway routes
+now direct administrators to Settings without exposing configuration syntax;
+dynamic gateway intake errors use the shared customer-safe filter. Focused local
+endpoint checks verify both SAML paths and both gateway error envelopes.
+
+Additional October 9 UX-292 follow-through: remote sign-in failures now show a
+branded recovery page, security-policy failures include a support reference,
+and Agent Factory's disabled job-description action points to Settings. The
+static error-copy guard now covers direct error JSON responses as well as raised
+HTTP errors; the scan and focused journeys pass locally. Other dynamic setup
+errors and direct instructions remain under review.
+
+October 9 GRC Studio evidence handoff follow-through: setup now retains the
+published design's exact ID, version, binding and integrity digests in the
+signed source review. Lightwork rechecks the same active published version and
+approved collector before review, displays the design in the complete setup
+review and source list, and preserves the origin when a source is edited. The
+source form now allows check discovery before any check is selected. Focused UI/API
+journeys and Ruff passed locally; no hosted CI or live provider call was used.
+The handoff still configures evidence collection only and does not write
+evidence into Studio records.
+
+October 9 UX-340 follow-through: the Tasks page and its live search now use the
+same reason-aware status words as Chat and recent conversations. Provider,
+workspace, policy, spending-limit and other terminal stops read “Stopped”; an
+open question reads “Needs your answer”; a prepared workflow pilot reads “Ready
+to start.” The Tasks filter test now executes the real page script and verifies
+that a stopped run is excluded from “Waiting for you.” Focused local page, API
+and browser-status checks passed. This does not infer an approval wait from a
+blocked task; approvals remain in their own review queue.
+
+October 9 UX-408 follow-through: SQLite and PostgreSQL now read the total and
+matching embedding-identity counts in one collection-scoped pass. Each search
+checks the collection before paying to embed a query, then verifies every
+returned hit against that identity; redundant full-collection rescans are
+removed. Focused local knowledge checks passed. A metadata GIN index and live
+pgvector query-plan qualification remain open; no hosted CI or paid model call
+was used.
+
+October 9 UX-257 follow-through: delivery leases now consume the bounded
+attempt budget when acquired, so a worker that repeatedly disappears cannot
+keep an alert in an infinite reclaim loop. An expired final lease is retained
+as dead-lettered with a safe reason code. Lease tokens use the injected random
+secret source and bind the exact tenant, delivery, worker and lease revision.
+Focused core, API and scheduler checks passed locally; no hosted CI or outside
+alert was sent.
+
+October 9 UX-264 follow-through: enabling event-triggered workflows in Settings
+now refreshes the live scheduler. The setting uses the `event_triggers` config
+section, which had been omitted from the existing runtime-refresh list; the
+dashboard now starts and reconciles its worker after the save. A focused local
+settings-route regression check passed; no hosted CI was run.
+
+October 9 UX-281 follow-through: restoring an older consent banner now rebuilds
+its exact historical rule group and content against the website or app's current
+settings. Lightwork verifies the source content before creating a new signed
+publication, so a property edit no longer makes a valid historical banner
+unrestorable and changed or missing source content still fails closed. The UI
+confirmation explains the current-settings check. Focused local publishing
+checks passed; no hosted CI or live customer publication was used.
+
+October 9 UX-306 follow-through: the older GRC pointer sender now supports
+ServiceNow only, and the installer, Settings All, and the module switch prevent
+it from overlapping the semantic ServiceNow sender. Both send paths check the
+effective configuration before any write, so older or hand-edited conflicting
+settings stop safely. The governance page explains the conflict, links to its
+settings, and disables dispatch. Focused local core, dashboard and installer
+checks passed; no hosted CI or vendor write was run.
+
+October 9 UX-288 follow-through: a dynamic route-parity check now exercises all
+12 customer-ready installation-wide pages against direct access, the page
+finder, the standard sidebar and the shared Chat-shell navigation. It checks
+workspace and installation roles independently, including the case where a
+workspace administrator must not see installation controls. The focused local
+route test passed; installed Windows and customer-session acceptance remain
+open.
+
+October 9 governance access follow-through: staff with read-only access can now
+open the AI governance workspace and review its bounded status records. Every
+edit control in the workspace is disabled for them, and the API still refuses
+write attempts; detailed assessment answers remain behind the higher access
+check. The local role journey covers workspace rendering, safe reads and refused
+writes.
+
+October 9 GRC Studio follow-through: questionnaire import now states its actual
+8-to-256 question limit without promising that every compliance topic is
+automatically covered. The connected-service screen explains when a destination
+can be reviewed but not sent to, and tells non-admins why the send action is
+disabled. Focused questionnaire and UI journeys passed locally; no hosted CI or
+connected-service write was used.
+
+October 9 workflow-authoring cost guard: one workflow-writing request can now
+dispatch at most one model completion, even if a future drafting path attempts
+to retry through the supplied completion function. The existing per-request
+spending limit and final dispatch checks still apply. Focused local admission
+checks passed; no hosted CI or paid model request was used.
+
+October 9 commitment-review follow-through: teams can now enter up to 32
+accepted orders in the page or guided Copilot form, including the reported
+material and shared work-center units each order needs. They can review
+combined resource needs, copy all orders into a fresh review, and record
+observed contention against any listed pair. The API
+preserves the existing two-order request shape for older clients, validates
+the 32-order ceiling, unique names and resource bounds, and uses stable order
+IDs. Empty additions preserve the original two-order request digest so older
+uncertain-save retries still recover their existing record. Focused Python API
+and JavaScript UI journeys passed locally; hosted CI and external services were
+not used.
+
+October 9 Copilot Learning follow-through: an installation administrator can
+enter a plain-language code-practice objective, choose bounded rounds, review
+the configured model and per-run spending limit, then start the same native
+isolated practice task with separate confirmation. The saved proposals and test
+results remain review-only; no code is installed automatically. A lost reply
+offers a read-only check of the original task, without another run. Focused
+service and actual Copilot DOM journeys passed locally; no hosted CI or paid
+model call was used.
+
+The Learning page now describes this as trying improvements to Lightwork,
+keeps editable source areas collapsed, and reports saved check pass rates with
+their sample counts. The page states that stronger practice checks do not prove
+better customer outcomes; no test score is presented as business impact.
+Completed runs describe the number of ideas considered and changes checked in
+each round without exposing internal evaluator notes by default.
+Each saved result now opens a read-only review with the before/after check rates
+and exact proposed change when retained. Lightwork verifies the saved change's
+content hash before showing it; missing or inconsistent source is not presented
+as a verified suggestion. When a retained change is too large to display, the
+review says that explicitly instead of implying the source was discarded.
+Before revealing source text, Lightwork also scans it for recognizable
+credentials and withholds it when sensitive content is detected or that check
+is unavailable. Opening a suggestion adds a deployment-wide Activity entry with
+the reviewer, suggestion reference and saved-change digest; patch text is never
+copied into the activity record. If that entry cannot be confirmed, the review
+does not return the suggestion.
